    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2025-01-08T14:43:49Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Enabling both 'mini.completion' and 'blink.cmp' is a bad idea: they both provide *auto*completion functionalities which will conflict with one another.\r\n\r\nWhat you're seeing as \"words from another buffer/file\" are indeed words from all loaded buffers (probably, only *listed* buffers, to be precise) and come from built-in Neovim (and Vim) completion. See `:h i_CTRL-N`. It is a highly built-in feature which doesn't have a good way of utilizing it other than actually simulating pressing `<C-n>`. It is also blocking/synchronous which has noticeable lagging when there is a very large buffer opened (like 10K - 100K lines depending on the machine).\r\n\r\nThe best way for 'blink.cmp' to support this kind of completion is through its own implementation as a different source.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Enabling both 'mini.completion' and 'blink.cmp' is a bad idea: they both provide <em>auto</em>completion functionalities which will conflict with one another.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">What you're seeing as \"words from another buffer/file\" are indeed words from all loaded buffers (probably, only <em>listed</em> buffers, to be precise) and come from built-in Neovim (and Vim) completion. See <code class=\"notranslate\">:h i_CTRL-N</code>. It is a highly built-in feature which doesn't have a good way of utilizing it other than actually simulating pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-n&gt;</code>. It is also blocking/synchronous which has noticeable lagging when there is a very large buffer opened (like 10K - 100K lines depending on the machine).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The best way for 'blink.cmp' to support this kind of completion is through its own implementation as a different source.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Enabling both 'mini.completion' and 'blink.cmp' is a bad idea: they both provide autocompletion functionalities which will conflict with one another.\nWhat you're seeing as \"words from another buffer/file\" are indeed words from all loaded buffers (probably, only listed buffers, to be precise) and come from built-in Neovim (and Vim) completion. See :h i_CTRL-N. It is a highly built-in feature which doesn't have a good way of utilizing it other than actually simulating pressing <C-n>. It is also blocking/synchronous which has noticeable lagging when there is a very large buffer opened (like 10K - 100K lines depending on the machine).\nThe best way for 'blink.cmp' to support this kind of completion is through its own implementation as a different source.",
